sections.Testkonzept/Auswertungsmodus:
The results on the factorial structure of the questionnaire suggest that both the two superordinate scales and the six individual scale scores can be used. For scoring the individual subscales, the responses to all items belonging to the respective subscale are averaged.
The values for the two superordinate scales "Anxiety about statistics" and "Attitude towards statistics" are formed by averaging the previously calculated values of the three corresponding subscales in each case:
Value total scale "Anxiety about statistics" = ((sum of all items of scale (a)/8) + (sum of all items of scale (b)/11) + (sum of all items of scale (c)/4))/3;
Value total scale "Attitude towards statistics" = ((sum of all items of scale (d)/7) + (sum of all items of scale (e)/15) + (sum of all items of scale (f)/6))/3.
The values for the six subscales and the two total scales can each range between 1 and 5.
Assignment of the items to the total scale "Anxiety about statistics":
a) Anxiety in statistics classes and statistics exams: Item01, Item04, Item08, Item11, Item13, Item15, Item21, Item22;
b) Anxiety about interpreting statistics: Item02, Item05, Item06, Item07, Item09, Item10, Item12, Item14, Item17, Item18, Item20;
c) Anxiety about asking for help: Item03, Item16, Item19, Item23.
Assignment of the items to the total scale "Attitude towards statistics":
d) Academic self-concept in statistics: Item25, Item31, Item34, Item38, Item39, Item48, Item51;
e) Value of statistics: Item24, Item26, Item27, Item28, Item29, Item33, Item35, Item36, Item37, Item40, Item41, Item42, Item45, Item49, Item50;
f) Attitude towards statistics instructors: Item30, Item32, Item43, Item44, Item46, Item47.
